// The compiler tells you once — at the definition, not N times at the call.
//
// `db_query` is a Heat built-in. An LLM that reaches for the name — to
// stub it (treating Heat like a language where the stdlib must be
// declared) or to write its own query helper — used to get a confusing
// "expects 3 argument(s), got 1" at every CALL site, because the call
// silently dispatched to the 3-arg built-in and ignored the user's
// 1-arg definition. Heat has no overloading, so the name can't be
// overridden; it's now refused at the DEFINITION with one clear message
// that names the cause and the fix.
//
// ❌ Expected: error[NL-0279] — does not produce a binary.

fn db_query(sql: String) -> Int [io]:
    print(value: sql)
    return 0

fn main() -> Int [io]:
    // ❌ NL-0279 fired at the definition above. Without it, this call
    // would dispatch to the built-in and report the built-in's arity.
    return db_query(sql: "SELECT 1")
